1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,850
The tallest building in the world stands on sand, not rock.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:08,350
It's the 828 m Burj Khalifa in Dubai.

3
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:12,800
Under it, there is no hard rock, only sand and soft ground.

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:17,800
So they put 192 concrete piles, each 1.5 m wide, into the ground.

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,700
Each pile is about 50 m long.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:25,450
A 3.7 m thick concrete slab covers them all.

7
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:29,800
The piles don't stand on the bottom. They hold the weight by gripping the soil around them.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,950
A tower almost 830 m tall stands by grabbing the ground.

9
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:38,567
Huge cargo ships climb stairs of water, like going over a mountain.

10
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:41,917
This is the Panama Canal, across the narrow middle of the Americas.

11
00:00:41,917 --> 00:00:46,117
In the middle of the canal, there is a man-made lake 26 m higher than the sea.

12
00:00:46,117 --> 00:00:51,017
A ship goes into a water room called a lock. The gates close, water fills in, and the ship rises.

13
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:56,067
They do this a few times to reach the lake, then go down on the other side.

14
00:00:56,067 --> 00:01:00,617
There are no pumps. Lake water flows down by gravity and lifts the ship.

15
00:01:00,617 --> 00:01:04,867
Each ship that passes uses about 200 million liters of fresh water.

16
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:09,167
So in dry years, fewer ships are allowed to pass.

17
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,900
Dubai made islands by 'spraying' sand into the sea.

18
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:17,850
From the sky, this man-made island looks like a palm tree. It's Palm Jumeirah.

19
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:21,700
In 2001, work started on empty sea.

20
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:26,650
Ships sucked up sand from the sea floor and sprayed it in the air like a rainbow.

21
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,000
They piled up about 100 million cubic meters of sand.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,400
The moon-shaped wall that stops the waves was built one big rock at a time.

23
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:39,200
Amazingly, the island has no concrete or steel at its base.

24
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,050
They built a city on the sea with only sand and rock.

25
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:50,867
In the middle of a U.S. desert, a giant 221 m concrete wall blocks a river.

26
00:01:50,867 --> 00:01:53,317
It's the Hoover Dam, built in the 1930s.

27
00:01:53,317 --> 00:01:57,217
But when concrete hardens, it makes a lot of heat by itself.

28
00:01:57,217 --> 00:02:02,667
If they poured it all at once, it would take 125 years to cool down.

29
00:02:02,667 --> 00:02:06,717
So they split the dam into many blocks and poured them piece by piece.

30
00:02:06,717 --> 00:02:10,517
Inside the blocks, they laid over 900 km of steel pipes.

31
00:02:10,517 --> 00:02:15,267
Cold water ran through them to cool the concrete from inside.

32
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:19,467
After it cooled, the pipes were filled with cement and became part of the dam.

33
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:25,000
Did you know the Leaning Tower of Pisa was once closed because it might fall?

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,350
The tower started to lean while it was still being built.

35
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:32,150
The ground is soft clay, so one side slowly sank.

36
00:02:32,150 --> 00:02:36,250
In 1990, it leaned too much, so they closed it.

37
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:40,250
Engineers took out tiny bits of soil from under the other side.

38
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:43,550
Then the tower slowly moved back by itself.

39
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:46,950
In 11 years, they straightened it by about 45 cm.

40
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:51,200
It still leans, but it should be fine for hundreds of years.

41
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:56,733
On the African grasslands, dirt towers rise many times taller than a person.

42
00:02:56,733 --> 00:03:00,733
Termites build them by mixing spit and dirt.

43
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:03,833
Big ones grow up to 8 m tall.

44
00:03:03,833 --> 00:03:08,533
But the termites don't live at the top. They live in a nest underground.

45
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:13,283
In the nest, they grow a fungus that is very sensitive to heat.

46
00:03:13,283 --> 00:03:19,433
So the tower has many chimney-like tunnels that move air with wind and heat.

47
00:03:19,433 --> 00:03:23,033
The whole tower is like a giant breathing lung.

48
00:03:23,033 --> 00:03:27,083
People are studying this design to make buildings that need less AC.

49
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:31,750
What shape appears if you pour cement into an empty ant nest?

50
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:34,750
The builders are leafcutter ants from South America.

51
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:39,600
People poured tons of cement into the hole, let it harden, then dug away the dirt.

52
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,850
What came out was a huge underground city.

53
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:47,300
It was up to 7 m deep, with up to 7,000 rooms.

54
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:51,250
In the rooms, the ants grow a fungus on cut leaves and eat it.

55
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:56,150
Food paths go 70 m long, and there are even air vents.

56
00:03:56,150 --> 00:04:00,550
Tiny ants built all of this with no plans at all.

57
00:04:01,567 --> 00:04:07,217
This heart weighs 180 kg and pumps 220 L of blood with each beat.

58
00:04:07,217 --> 00:04:10,867
It is the heart of the blue whale, the biggest animal on Earth.

59
00:04:10,867 --> 00:04:13,867
It's as big as a small car, or a pony.

60
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:18,467
A human heart is about 300 g, so this is about 600 times heavier.

61
00:04:18,467 --> 00:04:23,117
220 L of blood in one beat is more than a full bathtub.

62
00:04:23,117 --> 00:04:27,017
On a deep dive, this heart slows down to just 2 beats a minute.

63
00:04:27,017 --> 00:04:30,817
Back at the surface, it beats strongly again, over 30 times a minute.

64
00:04:30,817 --> 00:04:35,167
The biggest heart in Earth's history is still beating in the sea today.

65
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:40,000
At the end of a white glacier in Antarctica, blood-red water flows out.

66
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,600
It's Blood Falls, at Taylor Glacier.

67
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:47,050
Explorers first saw it in 1911. They thought red algae made the color.

68
00:04:47,050 --> 00:04:50,650
The real reason was very salty water trapped under the glacier.

69
00:04:50,650 --> 00:04:55,000
It's many times saltier than seawater, so it doesn't freeze below zero.

70
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,850
It's full of iron. When it meets air, it rusts and turns red.

71
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:04,400
When it comes out of the glacier, it's actually clear.

72
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,950
In that dark water, tiny life lives with no light and no oxygen.

73
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:13,483
Astronauts drink yesterday's coffee again today.

74
00:05:13,483 --> 00:05:16,683
On the International Space Station, they never throw away a drop of water.

75
00:05:16,683 --> 00:05:19,933
They collect water from sweat and breath out of the air.

76
00:05:19,933 --> 00:05:23,983
They boil urine into steam and take out only the clean water.

77
00:05:23,983 --> 00:05:29,283
For the leftover liquid, warm dry air squeezes out the last drops.

78
00:05:29,283 --> 00:05:34,183
In 2023, they reached 98% water recycling.

79
00:05:34,183 --> 00:05:38,283
After many filters, this water is said to be cleaner than tap water on Earth.

80
00:05:38,283 --> 00:05:41,633
We will need this to go to Mars one day.

81
00:05:42,633 --> 00:05:46,333
When a plane breaks into pieces, why is this box still OK?

82
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:50,083
It's called a black box, but it's really bright orange.

83
00:05:50,083 --> 00:05:54,533
It can survive a hit 3,400 times stronger than gravity.

84
00:05:54,533 --> 00:05:58,583
It can last 1 hour in a 1,100°C fire,

85
00:05:58,583 --> 00:06:01,283
and the water pressure 6,000 m under the sea.

86
00:06:01,283 --> 00:06:06,083
But the only part that really needs to be safe is the small recorder inside.

87
00:06:06,083 --> 00:06:11,533
So that small chip is wrapped in layers of heat shield and steel.

88
00:06:11,533 --> 00:06:16,333
If it falls into the sea, it sends signals for over 30 days, calling 'Find me!'
